Hi

I have written a small kernel module to test the latency of a rt-usermode task
triggered by interrupts. Currently the code is for x86 since it uses the rdtsc 
assembly instruction, to get cycle accurate timing values. The idea behind 
the module is to measure the latency it takes to handle an interrupt in 
usermode. It first reads the timestamp in the registered irq handler. 
Schedules a tasklet which also takes a timestamp and feeds these two 
timestamps out to the waiting usermode application with realtime priority 
which also in turn stores a timestamp. To avoid any buffering or io side 
effects a number of datasets is collected and finally printed out. Since i 
have a small possibility of a lost update i have also built in an overrun 
check to see if i have lost some interrupts.

Given the fact that the usermode task runs at realtime priority under the
2.6.29-rc6-rt3 (config attached, cool'n quiet off) kernel the timings have 
very large variations? 
(Besides there might be some still some problems with my implementation seen by 
negative reaction times?). If there are any better ideas to cut the latency
i would be interested to hear about them.

Best regards
Tim

PS: Why is udev not creating a device node as it should, when insmodding
    (you have to "mknod /dev/lmm c <fill in nr from /proc/devices here> 0" )
